  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Cook Islands Chamber of Commerce launched a Sole Traders Helpdesk as a service in response to members concerns about growth of the informal economy as a result of COVID-19. Among 495 registered sole traders operating in the informal economy, the service provided 257 with information on taxation and social security regulations and assistance to submit their documentation for registration in the national Superannuation Fund; and has provided training to sole traders on key areas of business planning and financial management. With a view to sustainability and impact, CICC successfully advocated for the Helpdesk to be included as a standing agenda item of the National Private Sector Taskforce, an advisory group to Government established to provide recommendations on COVID policy and programmatic responses. The inclusion of the Helpdesk in the agenda provided a platform for CICC to engage in regular advocacy in support of sole traders as part of COVID response and recovery plans. CICC also established and chaired an Advisory Group consisting of National Private Sector Taskforce members to monitor the progress of the Helpdesk and its achievements while providing guidance. The Advisory Group members include 3 private sector representatives from CICC Executive Committee and 4 representatives of the Ministry of Finance and Economic Management. The MFEM provided CICC with funds for human resources to be allocated to continue the social security related services of the Helpdesk until December 2021 with a possible renewal in 2022. A private bank also provided a grant for CICC to continue technical training to sole traders on key areas of business planning and financial management until December 2021. As a result of the Helpdesk, CICC recruited 12 new members. The remaining 245 sole traders have been added to CICCs CRM as potential members and will be targeted in future membership campaigns and for services, helping to extend membership to under-represented groups. The ILO provided assistance to the Chamber on the design of the business management and financial literacy curriculum for sole traders and in undertaking a feasibility assessment for the new sole trader&apos;s service with consideration of potential for sustainability. The ILO supported the development and production of information and awareness raising materials and of training materials, and assisted the service delivery to ensure inclusion of sole traders in outer islands. 
Further assistance included collaboration with the ITC-ILO to provide the Chamber with customized Customer Relationship Management (CRM) software. The ITC-ILO trained the Chamber&apos;s CEO and membership unit in June 2021 so they could use CRM software to improve the management and enhance the efficiencies of the organization. In particular, the training focused on enhancing CICC&apos;s capacity in the areas of building member relationships with those that accessed the sole trader&apos;s helpdesk, to increase general membership recruitment and sales of services, and to professionalize its financial management.</narrative>
   </description>
